Klaus, I spent some time playing Angband, another game in the "roguelike"
family, and liked it better than Nethack.  (Tastes vary, of course.)  One
nice thing about it is that it has an optional non-isometric graphical
view, for those of us who like that kind of thing, thouch you can play it
ASCII-style if you prefer.

The problem with this whole family of games, IMO, is that they become too
repetitive after your initial enthusiasm burns out.  I'd rather have
something indoor+outdoor, with a persistent world rather than an endless
sequence of random levels.

There's stuff like that out there, including some with graphical displays,
but I haven't found one I'm hot about yet.  I've recently tried Crossfire
and may try it some more, but IMO its UI isn't very well designed and it
demands you to attend low-level details that probably should have been
left out of the game.  It does incorporate some nice ideas though.

Wish I had time to write my own... just like I say about almost every
application I use.


In a different but related genre, I've been playing some Wesnoth lately.
It's a mostly outdoor strategy game with a heroic fantasy theme.  The
units are individual elves, dwarves, zombies, etc., with character classes
and mildy varying personal abilities within each class, and the ability to
advance just a few levels with experience.  As always, I would have done a
lot of stuff differently, but overall I give this one very high marks.


Angband: http://thangorodrim.net/

Crossfire: http://crossfire.real-time.com/

Wesnoth: http://www.wesnoth.org/index.htm N.B. - The graphics look better
than the screenshots suggest.  In fact, this is probably the most polished
open-source game I've played.

